1
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:11,017
Four machines here run on industrial PCs: real J1939 buses,
Sparkplug B over MQTT, into Abenix.

2
00:00:11,017 --> 00:00:18,017
A worker walks into EX1's swing zone. The hold crosses MQTT to EX1's PC,
and EX1 is still 249 ms after the twin saw the worker.

3
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:24,817
Every hop is measured, from the bus through the gateway and the broker into the Abenix store, and back.

4
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:29,833
Four machines run on their own industrial PCs, the rest are simulated. The labels say which: IPC · REAL BUS or SIM.

5
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:33,417
We unplug EX1's PC from the fault deck. It drops off MQTT without a goodbye.

6
00:00:34,617 --> 00:00:39,017
Abenix sees it die on the broker, and EX1's own safety PLC holds it. Its console and its card show the PC offline.

7
00:00:39,017 --> 00:00:42,017
Plugged back in, it is born again on
the broker and goes back to work.

8
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:46,833
On the left, EX1's controllers stream live from its PC again. Bottom left, every frame on its bus decoded, and every layer timed.

9
00:00:48,017 --> 00:00:49,433
A noisy harness on EX1, from the fault deck.

10
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:54,400
Error frames and retransmits on EX1's wire, each frame tagged IPC. The bus keeps going.

11
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:02,600
EX1's main pump starts to wear.
It works harder for the same flow, and the oil heats up.

12
00:01:10,883 --> 00:01:20,783
A rule in Abenix sees EX1's oil running hot, 15% above its own normal: 66.5 °C over the
last 5 minutes against 57.6 °C the half hour before. It wakes the maintenance tech.

13
00:01:21,683 --> 00:01:31,483
It asks to put EX1 in safe mode. A person decides in Abenix, and the site manager's phone shows the request.

14
00:01:32,483 --> 00:01:36,883
Approved in Abenix by Admin User, and EX1's PC takes it 0.5 s later: Mode/Set safe, confirmed on its bus.

15
00:01:37,483 --> 00:01:48,783
In Abenix every PC is a device, with live graphs, history and the raw frames.
